Solution:
$(1)$ The function $f(x)=|x+1|-|x-2|$,
$\therefore f(x)= \begin{cases} -3, & x < -1 \\ 2x-1, & -1\leqslant x\leqslant 2 \\ 3, & x > 2 \end{cases}$,
$\therefore$ When $x < -1$, $f(x)=-3$, which does not satisfy $f(x)\geqslant 1$;
When $-1\leqslant x\leqslant 2$, $f(x)=2x-1$, solving $2x-1\geqslant 1$ gives $x\geqslant 1$, $\therefore 1\leqslant x\leqslant 2$;
When $x > 2$, $f(x)=3$, which satisfies $f(x)\geqslant 1$;
